#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""conformance -- /standard's twelve items, measured (proposal 21, S-04).

  conformance [--project DIR] [--json]

The sponsor made the standard mandatory for every project (proposal 21,
A-01 to A-04), and a session reports "standard: N of 12 hold". That number
comes from here, not from a session's say-so. The contract is
skills/standard/SKILL.md, sections 2 and 3; each item below is the
mechanical rule this tool applies for it.

OUTPUT. The first line is `standard: N of 12 hold`, then one line per item:
`✓` holds, `✗` does not hold (why, then the fix), `…` waiting on
common-rules (what RULES is missing). Every value printed -- in the text and
in --json -- goes through bin/warmup's shown(), so a ledger id or a path
cannot forge a line or hide text. --json gives
{"project", "hold", "total": 12, "items": [{"n", "name", "state", "why", "fix"}]},
where state is "holds", "does not hold" or "waiting".

DIR is resolved to its git top level, as bin/warmup does.

THE TWELVE RULES

 1 Rules read and implemented. Holds when DIR is the rules repository
   itself: the same checkout as this tool, or a checkout sharing its git
   common directory (a worktree of it). rulecheck reports that case as
   "nothing to align against". Otherwise `.common-rules-version` must exist.
   When bin/rulecheck provides mandatory_pending() (S-09), it holds when the
   answer is `aligned`, or `behind` with no Standard change entry pending.
   Pending entries do not hold, naming their headings, and so does anything
   else it answers. Without mandatory_pending() it falls back to
   `rulecheck --quiet`: exit 0 holds; exit 1 (the stamp is behind) is
   waiting on common-rules (S-09), because without S-09 nothing can tell a
   mandatory change from information; any other exit does not hold.
 2 Migrated. tools/tracker/ledger.find() finds at least one ledger. Every
   ledger found parses and validate()s. Each ledger's tracker page is
   fresh: tools/tracker/render.freshness(), which is exactly what
   `tracker render --check` calls.
 3 Declared. `.common-rules.json` exists, tools/project.problems() is empty,
   and tools/project.declared() -- only what the file validly declares, no
   defaults and no `.common-rules-test` fallback -- has read_order,
   safety_rules, gates.quick and gates.merge. plan_check and plan_page are
   asked for only "when the project has its own generator", which no tool
   can see, so they are not checked.
 4 Installed. All of these:
   - the git pre-commit hook (`git rev-parse --git-path hooks`, which
     honours core.hooksPath) carries a `# derecord-body-sha256: <hex>` line
     whose value is the sha256 of everything after it -- derecord's own
     is_ours() rule;
   - `.claude/settings.json` has a SessionStart, a PreCompact and a Stop
     command for hooks/sessionstart, hooks/precompact and hooks/stop, and a
     PostToolUse command for hooks/posttooluse-agent. Each command must end
     in `/hooks/<name>` (derecord's own suffix rule), name an existing file,
     and sit in a checkout that holds bin/derecord. The matcher is not
     checked; derecord corrects it;
   - every line in bin/derecord's IGNORE_LINES (read from derecord, so the
     two cannot drift) is in `.gitignore` verbatim, or
     `git check-ignore -v -z --stdin --no-index` shows a `.gitignore` file inside
     the project ignoring its path, as written or as a directory, with a
     pattern that is not a negation. The PhotoVault app writes `.swarm/*`
     at the top and ignores `.claude-flow/data` from its own
     `.claude-flow/.gitignore`: the same paths, other spellings.
     `.git/info/exclude` and a personal core.excludesFile do not count,
     even one named .gitignore, because a clone would track the file;
   - `git ls-files -- <line>` lists nothing for any of those lines, the
     same pathspec derecord uses to warn.
 5 Lead prompt. The prompts are every regular *lead-prompt*.md file at most
   three levels under docs/ (derecord's `find docs -maxdepth 3`, which
   covers docs/handovers/), not under a `superseded` folder. There must be
   at least one. When RULES' templates/lead-prompt.md does not carry
   `<!-- common-rules:lead-prompt proposal/21 -->` the item is waiting on
   common-rules (S-06). Otherwise every prompt must carry that marker.
 6 The ledger speaks the common language. For every ledger:
   - every blocked row has `owner`, and so does every open ask of kind
     decision;
   - every row has a non-empty `tag`;
   - exactly one of the keys `tiers` and `model_routing` is present;
   - the problems validate() finds with `requests` are none: validate()
     of the ledger minus validate() of the ledger without `requests`.
   Offending ids are named, the first five of each kind. No ledger does not
   hold.
 7 Proposals. `bin/proposalcheck --project DIR` exits 0. Exit 2, no
   docs/proposals, does not hold.
 8 No hand-kept duplicates. Holds when .common-rules.json validly declares
   plan_page. Otherwise, for every ledger NN-slug.json, a sibling
   NN-slug.md either does not exist, or has at most 5 lines and contains
   the ledger's file name. No ledger: nothing to duplicate, so it holds.
 9 Ruflo around items -- a PROXY. No file proves the Ruflo loop ran around
   each item; these are the traces it leaves. The item holds when any of
   these is true, checked in order:
   - a ledger records switches.ruflo off (reported as "switched off by ...");
   - there is Ruflo memory state: .swarm/memory.db, or any *.db in .swarm/,
     in DIR or in its main checkout. The database lives only in the main
     checkout, never in a worktree;
   - a row in progress or done has a log entry whose event or evidence
     mentions "ruflo", case-insensitive.
10 Publishing recorded. Every docs/proposals/tracker/*.published.json is
   well-formed. One beside its ledger is read by bin/warmup's own
   publish_state(), and a problem it names does not hold. One without a
   ledger is read with tools/tracker/render.published_problems(), and never
   through a symlink or outside DIR. "Page changed since publish" is a
   to-do, not a failure. No sidecar holds.
11 CLAUDE.md pointer. CLAUDE.md contains the `<!-- common-rules:warmup -->`
   ... `<!-- /common-rules:warmup -->` block, and the block equals
   tools/migrate.pointer_block() for the read order tools/project.load()
   gives. CLAUDE.md must be tracked at HEAD, and when
   `git diff HEAD -- CLAUDE.md` is not empty, the block at HEAD must equal
   the block on disk. That is: the uncommitted diff does not touch the
   block. Staged but uncommitted counts as uncommitted. The diff runs as its
   plumbing form, `git diff-index -p HEAD`, because porcelain `git diff`
   rewrites .git/index when the stat data is stale.
12 Card. `bin/warmup --project DIR --check --no-recall` exits 0.

READ-ONLY. Nothing here writes, and it runs only three tools, each confirmed
read-only from its code:
  - `rulecheck --quiet`, without --align;
  - `proposalcheck`;
  - `warmup --check --no-recall`, with no --state or --migrate; its own
    rulecheck run is --quiet.
Every git and child process runs with GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0, so a
`git diff` or a warmup `git log` never refreshes .git/index; and with
PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1. This tool's own git calls add
`-c core.fsmonitor=false`, `--no-ext-diff` and `--no-textconv`: a project's
config or .gitattributes must not make a read run a command.
tests/test_conformance.py snapshots every byte and mtime of a project,
.git included, around a run.

Exit codes:
  0  no item does not hold. A waiting item does not make it 1: what is
     missing is in common-rules, not the project.
  1  any item does not hold.
  2  could not run: DIR is not inside a git repository, or bin/warmup
     could not be loaded.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import hashlib
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path, PurePosixPath
from typing import NamedTuple

sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
RULES_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(RULES_DIR))

from tools import migrate as M  # noqa: E402
from tools import project as P  # noqa: E402
from tools.tracker import ledger as L  # noqa: E402
from tools.tracker import render as R  # noqa: E402

HOLDS, NOT, WAITING = "holds", "does not hold", "waiting"
TOTAL = 12
TIMEOUT = 900
STAMP = ".common-rules-version"
LEAD_TEMPLATE = RULES_DIR / "templates" / "lead-prompt.md"
LEAD_MARKER = "<!-- common-rules:lead-prompt proposal/21 -->"
HOOK_MARKER = re.compile(r"^# derecord-body-sha256: ([0-9a-f]{64})$")
SETTINGS_HOOKS = (("SessionStart", "sessionstart"), ("PreCompact", "precompact"),
                  ("Stop", "stop"), ("PostToolUse", "posttooluse-agent"))
FIRST = 5


def _load(name: str, path: Path):
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(name, str(path))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader)
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


try:
    W = _load("_conformance_warmup", RULES_DIR / "bin" / "warmup")
except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 -- any failure to load is "could not run", reported by main()
    W = None


def _mandatory_pending():
    """rulecheck.mandatory_pending (S-09) when this checkout's rulecheck has it, else None."""
    try:
        module = _load("_conformance_rulecheck", RULES_DIR / "bin" / "rulecheck")
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        return None
    fn = getattr(module, "mandatory_pending", None)
    return fn if callable(fn) else None


MANDATORY_PENDING = _mandatory_pending()


def shown(value) -> str:
    return W.shown(value)


class Result(NamedTuple):
    # A NamedTuple, not a dataclass: @dataclass looks its module up in
    # sys.modules, and this file is loaded by path (no .py), where it is not.
    state: str
    why: str = ""
    fix: str = ""


def holds(why: str = "") -> Result:
    return Result(HOLDS, why, "")


def not_hold(why: str, fix: str) -> Result:
    return Result(NOT, why, fix)


def waiting(why: str, fix: str = "") -> Result:
    return Result(WAITING, why, fix)


class CouldNotRun(Exception):
    pass


def _env() -> dict:
    return dict(os.environ, GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS="0", PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE="1")


def run(cmd: list, input: str | None = None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess | str:
    """The finished process, or a sentence saying why it did not finish."""
    feed = {"input": input} if input is not None else {"stdin": subprocess.DEVNULL}
    try:
        return subprocess.run([str(c) for c in cmd], capture_output=True, text=True, errors="replace",
                              check=False, timeout=TIMEOUT, env=_env(), **feed)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return f"{Path(str(cmd[1] if cmd[0] == sys.executable else cmd[0])).name} did not finish in {TIMEOUT} s"
    except OSError as exc:
        return f"{Path(str(cmd[0])).name} could not be started ({type(exc).__name__})"


def _joined(values: list, cap: int = FIRST) -> str:
    head = ", ".join(str(v) for v in values[:cap])
    return head + (f" (+{len(values) - cap} more)" if len(values) > cap else "")


def _first_line(text: str) -> str:
    return next((l.strip() for l in text.splitlines() if l.strip()), "")


class Context:
    def __init__(self, root: Path):
        self.root = Path(root)
        self._ledgers: list | None = None

    def git(self, *args: str, input: str | None = None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess | str:
        return run(["git", "-c", "core.fsmonitor=false", "-C", self.root, *args], input=input)

    def git_out(self, *args: str, input: str | None = None) -> str | None:
        r = self.git(*args, input=input)
        return r.stdout if isinstance(r, subprocess.CompletedProcess) and r.returncode == 0 else None

    def rel(self, path: Path) -> str:
        try:
            return str(Path(path).relative_to(self.root))
        except ValueError:
            return str(path)

    def ledgers(self) -> list[tuple[Path, dict | None, str | None]]:
        """(path, the ledger or None, why it could not be read or None), in find() order."""
        if self._ledgers is None:
            out = []
            for p in L.find(self.root):
                try:
                    data = L.load(p)
                except (OSError, ValueError):
                    out.append((p, None, "is not valid JSON"))
                    continue
                if not isinstance(data, dict):
                    out.append((p, None, "is not a JSON object"))
                    continue
                out.append((p, data, None))
            self._ledgers = out
        return self._ledgers

    def declared(self) -> dict:
        try:
            return P.declared(self.root)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            return {}


def common_dir(path: Path) -> Path | None:
    r = run(["git", "-C", path, "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir"])
    if not isinstance(r, subprocess.CompletedProcess) or r.returncode != 0 or not r.stdout.strip():
        return None
    p = Path(r.stdout.strip())
    return (p if p.is_absolute() else Path(path) / p).resolve()


def toplevel(project: Path) -> Path | None:
    if not Path(project).is_dir():
        return None
    r = run(["git", "-C", project, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])
    if not isinstance(r, subprocess.CompletedProcess) or r.returncode != 0 or not r.stdout.strip():
        return None
    return Path(r.stdout.strip())


# --- 1 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def is_rules_repository(root: Path) -> bool:
    if Path(root).resolve() == RULES_DIR.resolve():
        return True
    mine, theirs = common_dir(RULES_DIR), common_dir(root)
    return mine is not None and mine == theirs


def check_rules(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    root = ctx.root
    if is_rules_repository(root):
        return holds("this is the rules repository itself -- rulecheck: nothing to align against")
    align = f"{RULES_DIR}/bin/rulecheck --project {root} --align"
    if not (root / STAMP).is_file():
        return not_hold(f"{STAMP} is absent -- the project has never recorded which rules it follows",
                        f"read common-rules' CHANGELOG.md, implement every Standard change entry, then run "
                        f"{align} and commit the stamp")
    fn = MANDATORY_PENDING
    if fn is not None:
        try:
            p = fn(root)
        except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001
            return not_hold(f"rulecheck's mandatory_pending() failed ({type(exc).__name__})",
                            f"run {RULES_DIR}/bin/rulecheck --project {root} --mandatory and fix what it names")
        state = getattr(p, "state", None)
        entries = tuple(getattr(p, "entries", ()) or ())
        if state == "aligned":
            return holds(f"stamp aligned with common-rules {getattr(p, 'current', '')}")
        if state == "behind" and not entries:
            return holds(f"stamp {getattr(p, 'stamp', '')} is behind {getattr(p, 'current', '')} on information "
                         f"only -- no mandatory Standard change pending")
        if state in ("behind", "no stamp") and entries:
            headings = [getattr(e, "heading", "?") for e in entries]
            return not_hold(f"{len(entries)} mandatory Standard change(s) pending since the stamp: "
                            f"{_joined(headings, 3)}",
                            f"implement each entry and record it in the ledger (skills/standard section 3), "
                            f"then run {align} and commit the stamp")
        if state == "could not check":
            return not_hold(f"rulecheck could not check the stamp: {getattr(p, 'why', '')}",
                            f"re-read the rules in full, then run {align} and commit the stamp")
        return not_hold(f"rulecheck answers {state!r} for a project with a stamp",
                        f"point CLAUDE.md at CLAUDE-workflow.md, then run {align}")
    r = run([sys.executable, RULES_DIR / "bin" / "rulecheck", "--project", root, "--quiet"])
    if isinstance(r, str):
        return not_hold(r, f"run {RULES_DIR}/bin/rulecheck --project {root}")
    if r.returncode == 0:
        return holds("stamp aligned (rulecheck)")
    if r.returncode == 1:
        return waiting("the stamp is behind common-rules, and this rulecheck cannot yet tell a mandatory "
                       "Standard change from information: mandatory_pending() (S-09) is not in RULES",
                       f"read what changed with {RULES_DIR}/bin/rulecheck --project {root}; this re-measures "
                       f"once S-09 is merged")
    return not_hold(f"rulecheck could not check the stamp (exit {r.returncode}): {_first_line(r.stdout + r.stderr)}",
                    f"re-read the rules in full, then run {align} and commit the stamp")


# --- 2 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def check_migrated(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    ledgers = ctx.ledgers()
    migrate = f"{RULES_DIR}/bin/warmup --project {ctx.root} --migrate --dry-run --at <now>, then without --dry-run"
    if not ledgers:
        return not_hold("no ledger under docs/proposals (docs/proposals/NN-*.json with an items list)",
                        f"run {migrate}; a plan that is not a ledger yet becomes one with tracker import")
    bad = []
    for path, data, err in ledgers:
        rel = ctx.rel(path)
        if err:
            bad.append(f"{rel} {err}")
            continue
        problems = L.validate(data)
        if problems:
            bad.append(f"{rel}: {len(problems)} problem(s), first: {problems[0]}")
            continue
        page = R.default_out(path)
        fresh = R.freshness(path, page)
        if fresh != "ok":
            bad.append(f"page {ctx.rel(page)} is {fresh}")
    if bad:
        return not_hold("; ".join(bad[:3]) + (f" (+{len(bad) - 3} more)" if len(bad) > 3 else ""),
                        f"fix what {RULES_DIR}/bin/tracker validate names, then run {RULES_DIR}/bin/tracker "
                        f"render <ledger> (the pre-commit hook does it on commit)")
    return holds(f"{len(ledgers)} ledger(s), each valid, each page matching")


# --- 3 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def check_declared(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    if not (ctx.root / P.FILE).is_file():
        return not_hold(f"{P.FILE} is absent",
                        f"declare read_order, safety_rules, gates.quick and gates.merge in {P.FILE}")
    problems = P.problems(ctx.root)
    if problems:
        return not_hold(f"{P.FILE} has {len(problems)} problem(s), first: {problems[0]}",
                        f"fix {P.FILE}; {RULES_DIR}/bin/warmup --check names every problem")
    own = ctx.declared()
    missing = [k for k in ("read_order", "safety_rules") if k not in own]
    missing += [f"gates.{g}" for g in ("quick", "merge") if g not in own.get("gates", {})]
    if missing:
        return not_hold(f"{P.FILE} does not declare {', '.join(missing)}",
                        f"declare {', '.join(missing)} in {P.FILE}")
    return holds("declares read_order, safety_rules, gates.quick and gates.merge")


# --- 4 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def hook_is_derecords(path: Path) -> bool:
    """derecord's is_ours(): a marker line whose value is the sha256 of the body after it."""
    lines = path.read_text(errors="replace").split("\n")
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        m = HOOK_MARKER.match(line)
        if m:
            return hashlib.sha256("\n".join(lines[i + 1:]).encode()).hexdigest() == m.group(1)
    return False


def derecord_ignore_lines() -> list[str]:
    text = (RULES_DIR / "bin" / "derecord").read_text(errors="replace")
    m = re.search(r"^IGNORE_LINES=\(\n(.*?)^\)", text, re.M | re.S)
    return re.findall(r'"([^"\n]+)"', m.group(1)) if m else []


def ignored_by_gitignore(ctx: Context, line: str) -> bool:
    """git ignores the line's path -- as written, or as a directory -- by a
    .gitignore file inside the project, with a pattern that is not a negation.
    .git/info/exclude and a personal core.excludesFile are this machine's, not
    the project's: a clone would track the file, so neither counts. -z, so a
    source or path with a colon or quoting cannot be misread -- and git
    refuses -z without --stdin ("-z only makes sense with --stdin", exit 128),
    so the path goes in on stdin, NUL-terminated, never as an argument."""
    for probe in dict.fromkeys((line, line.rstrip("/") + "/")):
        out = ctx.git_out("check-ignore", "-v", "-z", "--stdin", "--no-index", input=probe + "\0")
        fields = (out or "").split("\0")
        if len(fields) < 4:
            continue
        source, pattern = fields[0], fields[2]
        where = PurePosixPath(source)
        if (source and not where.is_absolute() and ".." not in where.parts and where.name == ".gitignore"
                and not where.parts[0] == ".git" and not pattern.startswith("!")):
            return True
    return False


def check_installed(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    root = ctx.root
    problems: list[str] = []
    derecord = f"{RULES_DIR}/bin/derecord {root}"
    fixes = [f"run {derecord}"]

    hooks_dir = ctx.git_out("rev-parse", "--git-path", "hooks")
    hook = None
    if hooks_dir and hooks_dir.strip():
        hook = Path(hooks_dir.strip())
        hook = (hook if hook.is_absolute() else root / hook) / "pre-commit"
    if hook is None or not hook.is_file():
        problems.append("no git pre-commit hook")
    elif not hook_is_derecords(hook):
        problems.append("the git pre-commit hook is not derecord's, or its body no longer matches its "
                        "# derecord-body-sha256: line")

    settings_path = root / ".claude" / "settings.json"
    settings = None
    if not settings_path.is_file():
        problems.append("no .claude/settings.json")
    else:
        try:
            settings = json.loads(settings_path.read_text())
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            problems.append(".claude/settings.json is not valid JSON")
    if settings is not None:
        hooks = settings.get("hooks") if isinstance(settings, dict) else None
        hooks = hooks if isinstance(hooks, dict) else {}
        for event, name in SETTINGS_HOOKS:
            commands = [h.get("command") for e in (hooks.get(event) or []) if isinstance(e, dict)
                        for h in (e.get("hooks") or []) if isinstance(h, dict)]
            ok = False
            for c in commands:
                if isinstance(c, str) and c.strip().endswith(f"/hooks/{name}"):
                    target = Path(c.strip())
                    if target.is_file() and (target.parent.parent / "bin" / "derecord").is_file():
                        ok = True
                        break
            if not ok:
                problems.append(f"no {event} hook running common-rules' hooks/{name}")

    lines = derecord_ignore_lines()
    if not lines:
        return waiting("bin/derecord no longer declares IGNORE_LINES where conformance reads them",
                       "nothing to do in the project; common-rules must reconcile the two")
    gi = root / ".gitignore"
    present = {l.strip() for l in gi.read_text(errors="replace").splitlines()} if gi.is_file() else set()
    missing = [l for l in lines if l not in present and not ignored_by_gitignore(ctx, l)]
    if missing:
        problems.append(f".gitignore does not ignore {_joined(missing)}")

    tracked: list[str] = []
    for line in lines:
        out = ctx.git_out("ls-files", "--", line.rstrip("/"))
        tracked += [t for t in (out or "").splitlines() if t]
    tracked = sorted(set(tracked))
    if tracked:
        problems.append(f"Ruflo runtime file(s) tracked: {_joined(tracked)}")
        fixes.append("untrack them, after asking the sponsor (the files stay on disk): git rm -r --cached "
                     + " ".join(tracked[:FIRST]))
    if problems:
        return not_hold("; ".join(problems), "; ".join(fixes))
    return holds("derecord's pre-commit hook, checkpoint and agent hooks, and Ruflo ignore lines are in place")


# --- 5 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def lead_prompts(root: Path) -> list[Path]:
    docs = root / "docs"
    found = []
    if not docs.is_dir():
        return found
    for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(docs, followlinks=False):
        depth = len(Path(dirpath).relative_to(docs).parts)
        dirnames[:] = [d for d in dirnames if d != "superseded" and depth < 2]
        for name in filenames:
            p = Path(dirpath) / name
            if (re.fullmatch(r".*lead-prompt.*\.md", name) and p.is_file() and not p.is_symlink()
                    and P.inside(root, str(p.relative_to(root)))):
                found.append(p)
    return sorted(found)


def check_lead_prompt(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    prompts = lead_prompts(ctx.root)
    template = f"{RULES_DIR}/templates/lead-prompt.md"
    if not prompts:
        return not_hold("no lead prompt under docs/ (docs/handovers/*lead-prompt*.md, or *lead-prompt*.md up to "
                        "three levels down)",
                        f"regenerate it from {template}; {RULES_DIR}/bin/derecord seeds docs/handovers/lead-prompt.md")
    try:
        carries = LEAD_MARKER in Path(LEAD_TEMPLATE).read_text(errors="replace")
    except OSError:
        carries = False
    if not carries:
        return waiting(f"RULES' templates/lead-prompt.md does not carry {LEAD_MARKER} yet (S-06), so a prompt "
                       f"regenerated from it cannot be told from an older one",
                       "nothing to do in the project yet; this re-measures once S-06 is merged")
    missing = [ctx.rel(p) for p in prompts if LEAD_MARKER not in p.read_text(errors="replace")]
    if missing:
        return not_hold(f"{_joined(missing, 3)} does not carry {LEAD_MARKER} -- not regenerated from the template",
                        f"regenerate it from {template}, carry the project's own rules into its sections, and keep "
                        f"the old prompt under docs/handovers/superseded/")
    return holds(f"{len(prompts)} lead prompt(s), each from the template")


# --- 6 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def _request_problems(data: dict) -> list[str]:
    without = {k: v for k, v in data.items() if k != "requests"}
    other = set(L.validate(without))
    return [p for p in L.validate(data) if p not in other]


def check_language(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    ledgers = ctx.ledgers()
    if not ledgers:
        return not_hold("no ledger to speak it", "migrate first (item 2)")
    parts = []
    for path, data, err in ledgers:
        rel = ctx.rel(path)
        if err:
            parts.append(f"{rel} {err}")
            continue
        rows = [i for i in (data.get("items") or []) if isinstance(i, dict)]
        asks = [a for a in (data.get("asks") or []) if isinstance(a, dict)]

        def ident(x, n, kind):
            return x.get("id") if x.get("id") else f"{kind}[{n}]"

        blocked = [ident(i, n, "items") for n, i in enumerate(rows) if i.get("status") == "blocked" and not i.get("owner")]
        open_asks = [ident(a, n, "asks") for n, a in enumerate(asks)
                     if a.get("state") == "open" and a.get("kind") == "decision" and not a.get("owner")]
        untagged = [ident(i, n, "items") for n, i in enumerate(rows)
                    if not (isinstance(i.get("tag"), str) and i["tag"].strip())]
        tables = [k for k in ("tiers", "model_routing") if k in data]
        requests = _request_problems(data)
        mine = []
        if blocked:
            mine.append(f"blocked without owner: {_joined(blocked)}")
        if open_asks:
            mine.append(f"open decision asks without owner: {_joined(open_asks)}")
        if untagged:
            mine.append(f"rows without tag: {_joined(untagged)}")
        if len(tables) != 1:
            mine.append("two routing tables, tiers and model_routing" if tables else
                        "no routing table (tiers or model_routing)")
        if requests:
            mine.append(f"requests: {_joined(requests, 3)}")
        if mine:
            parts.append(f"{rel}: " + "; ".join(mine))
    if parts:
        return not_hold(" | ".join(parts),
                        "give every blocked row and open decision ask an owner (sponsor, lead or session:<name>), "
                        "every row its [ruflo · tier · model] tag, keep exactly one routing table, and fix the "
                        "requests tracker validate names")
    return holds(f"{len(ledgers)} ledger(s): owners, tags, one routing table, requests valid")


# --- 7 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def check_proposals(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    r = run([sys.executable, RULES_DIR / "bin" / "proposalcheck", "--project", ctx.root])
    fix = f"fix what {RULES_DIR}/bin/proposalcheck --project {ctx.root} names; create proposals with bin/new-proposal"
    if isinstance(r, str):
        return not_hold(r, fix)
    if r.returncode == 0:
        return holds(_first_line(r.stdout.splitlines()[-1] if r.stdout.strip() else ""))
    if r.returncode == 2:
        return not_hold("no docs/proposals/ -- proposalcheck has nothing to check",
                        f"create proposals with {RULES_DIR}/bin/new-proposal")
    last = [l for l in r.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip()]
    count = last[-1].strip() if last else f"exit {r.returncode}"
    return not_hold(f"proposalcheck: {_first_line(r.stdout) or _first_line(r.stderr)} ({count})", fix)


# --- 8 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def check_duplicates(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    if "plan_page" in ctx.declared():
        return holds("declares plan_page -- its page is generated, not hand-kept")
    twins = []
    for path, _data, _err in ctx.ledgers():
        md = path.with_suffix(".md")
        if not (md.exists() or md.is_symlink()):
            continue
        text = ""
        if md.is_file() and not md.is_symlink() and P.inside(ctx.root, ctx.rel(md)):
            text = md.read_text(errors="replace")
        if len(text.splitlines()) <= 5 and path.name in text:
            continue
        twins.append(ctx.rel(md))
    if twins:
        return not_hold(f"{_joined(twins)} mirror(s) a ledger by hand: more than 5 lines, or not naming the ledger file",
                        "retire each to a pointer of at most 5 lines naming its ledger file, or declare plan_page")
    return holds("no hand-kept twin of a ledger")


# --- 9 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def check_ruflo(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    ledgers = [(p, d) for p, d, err in ctx.ledgers() if not err]
    for path, data in ledgers:
        if not L.switch_on(data, "ruflo"):
            sw = data["switches"]["ruflo"]
            return holds(f"switched off by {sw.get('by')} at {sw.get('at')} ({ctx.rel(path)})")
    bases = [ctx.root]
    common = common_dir(ctx.root)
    if common is not None and common.name == ".git" and common.parent not in bases:
        bases.append(common.parent)
    for base in bases:
        swarm = base / ".swarm"
        try:
            has_db = (swarm / "memory.db").is_file() or (swarm.is_dir() and any(
                p.suffix == ".db" and p.is_file() for p in swarm.iterdir()))
        except OSError:
            has_db = False
        if has_db:
            return holds(f"Ruflo memory state in {swarm} (a proxy: the loop's trace, not proof it ran per item)")
    for path, data in ledgers:
        for row in (data.get("items") or []):
            if not isinstance(row, dict) or row.get("status") not in ("in progress", "done"):
                continue
            for entry in (row.get("log") or []):
                if isinstance(entry, dict) and any("ruflo" in str(entry.get(k) or "").lower()
                                                   for k in ("event", "evidence")):
                    return holds(f"{row.get('id')} in {ctx.rel(path)} logs a Ruflo step (a proxy)")
    return not_hold("no Ruflo trace -- a proxy, not proof: switches.ruflo is on, there is no .swarm/ memory "
                    "database, and no row in progress or done logs a ruflo step",
                    f"run {RULES_DIR}/bin/ruflo-item start | done | note | recall around every item, with RUFLO "
                    f"and RUFLO_NAMESPACE set")


# --- 10 ----------------------------------------------------------------------

def check_publishing(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    tracker = ctx.root / "docs" / "proposals" / "tracker"
    sidecars = sorted(tracker.glob("*.published.json")) if tracker.is_dir() else []
    if not sidecars:
        return holds("no published page recorded")
    bad = []
    for sidecar in sidecars:
        stem = sidecar.name[:-len(".published.json")]
        ledger = ctx.root / "docs" / "proposals" / f"{stem}.json"
        data = None
        if ledger.is_file():
            try:
                data = L.load(ledger)
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                data = None
        if isinstance(data, dict):
            _pub, problem, _label = W.publish_state(ctx.root, ledger, data, R.default_out(ledger))
            if problem:
                bad.append(problem)
            continue
        rel = ctx.rel(sidecar)
        if sidecar.is_symlink() or not P.inside(ctx.root, rel):
            bad.append(f"{rel} is a symlink or resolves outside the project -- not read")
            continue
        try:
            record = json.loads(sidecar.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, ValueError, RecursionError) as exc:
            bad.append(f"{rel} is malformed ({type(exc).__name__})")
            continue
        problems = R.published_problems(record)
        if problems:
            bad.append(f"{rel} is malformed: {'; '.join(problems)}")
    if bad:
        return not_hold("; ".join(bad[:3]) + (f" (+{len(bad) - 3} more)" if len(bad) > 3 else ""),
                        f"republish the page and record it with {RULES_DIR}/bin/tracker published <ledger> --url "
                        f"<url> (--page <path> for a declared plan_page), then commit the sidecar on its own")
    return holds(f"{len(sidecars)} publish record(s), each well-formed")


# --- 11 ----------------------------------------------------------------------

def _block(text: str) -> str | None:
    if M.BEGIN not in text or M.END not in text:
        return None
    a = text.index(M.BEGIN)
    end = text.find(M.END, a)
    return text[a:end + len(M.END)] if end >= 0 else None


def check_pointer(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    migrate = f"{RULES_DIR}/bin/warmup --project {ctx.root} --migrate"
    commit = "show the sponsor the diff, then commit the pointer block (/standard authorizes that and nothing else in CLAUDE.md)"
    cm = ctx.root / "CLAUDE.md"
    if not cm.is_file():
        return not_hold("no CLAUDE.md", f"run {migrate}; it writes the warm-up pointer block; {commit}")
    block = _block(cm.read_text(errors="replace"))
    if block is None:
        return not_hold(f"CLAUDE.md has no {M.BEGIN} block", f"run {migrate}; {commit}")
    if block != M.pointer_block(P.load(ctx.root)["read_order"]):
        return not_hold("the warm-up block in CLAUDE.md is not the one migrate writes for the declared read order",
                        f"run {migrate} to rewrite it; {commit}")
    if ctx.git_out("ls-files", "--error-unmatch", "--", "CLAUDE.md") is None:
        return not_hold("CLAUDE.md is not committed", commit)
    # `git diff HEAD`, as plumbing: porcelain `git diff` refreshes .git/index
    # and writes it back when the stat data is stale (a fresh clone or copy),
    # and GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 does not stop it -- measured by
    # tests/test_conformance.py's snapshot. diff-index never writes the index.
    # A stat-only difference it reports is harmless: the block comparison
    # below decides.
    diff = ctx.git_out("diff-index", "-p", "--no-ext-diff", "--no-textconv", "--no-color", "HEAD", "--", "CLAUDE.md")
    if diff is None:
        return not_hold("CLAUDE.md cannot be compared with HEAD", commit)
    if diff.strip():
        at_head = ctx.git_out("show", "--no-textconv", "HEAD:CLAUDE.md")
        if at_head is None or _block(at_head) != block:
            return not_hold("the warm-up block in CLAUDE.md has an uncommitted change", commit)
    return holds("CLAUDE.md carries the committed warm-up block for the declared read order")


# --- 12 ----------------------------------------------------------------------

def check_card(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    fix = f"run {RULES_DIR}/bin/warmup --project {ctx.root} --check and fix each problem it names"
    r = run([sys.executable, RULES_DIR / "bin" / "warmup", "--project", ctx.root, "--check", "--no-recall"])
    if isinstance(r, str):
        return not_hold(r, fix)
    if r.returncode == 0:
        return holds("warmup --check: ready")
    lines = r.stdout.splitlines()
    summary = next((l.strip() for l in lines if l.startswith("warmup --check:")), f"exit {r.returncode}")
    at = next((i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith("warmup --check:")), len(lines))
    first = next((l.strip()[2:] for l in lines[at + 1:] if l.strip().startswith("✗ ")), _first_line(r.stderr))
    return not_hold(f"{summary}" + (f", first: {first}" if first else ""), fix)


ITEMS = (
    (1, "Rules read and implemented", check_rules),
    (2, "Migrated", check_migrated),
    (3, "Declared", check_declared),
    (4, "Installed", check_installed),
    (5, "Lead prompt", check_lead_prompt),
    (6, "The ledger speaks the common language", check_language),
    (7, "Proposals", check_proposals),
    (8, "No hand-kept duplicates", check_duplicates),
    (9, "Ruflo around items", check_ruflo),
    (10, "Publishing recorded", check_publishing),
    (11, "CLAUDE.md pointer", check_pointer),
    (12, "Card", check_card),
)


def measure(project: Path) -> dict:
    """The report, every string already escaped. Raises CouldNotRun."""
    root = toplevel(Path(project))
    if root is None:
        raise CouldNotRun(f"{project} is not inside a git repository")
    ctx = Context(root)
    items = []
    for n, name, check in ITEMS:
        try:
            result = check(ctx)
        except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 -- one broken check must not hide the other eleven
            result = not_hold(f"the check itself failed ({type(exc).__name__}: {exc})",
                              "report this to common-rules; the item cannot be shown to hold")
        items.append({"n": n, "name": name, "state": result.state, "why": shown(result.why), "fix": shown(result.fix)})
    return {"project": shown(root), "hold": sum(1 for i in items if i["state"] == HOLDS),
            "total": TOTAL, "items": items}


def exit_code(data: dict) -> int:
    return 1 if any(i["state"] == NOT for i in data["items"]) else 0


def text(data: dict) -> str:
    out = [f"standard: {data['hold']} of {data['total']} hold"]
    for i in data["items"]:
        head = f"{i['n']} {i['name']}"
        if i["state"] == HOLDS:
            out.append(f"✓ {head}" + (f": {i['why']}" if i["why"] else ""))
        elif i["state"] == WAITING:
            out.append(f"… {head}: waiting on common-rules -- {i['why']}" + (f" -- {i['fix']}" if i["fix"] else ""))
        else:
            out.append(f"✗ {head}: {i['why']} -- fix: {i['fix']}")
    return "\n".join(out)


def main(argv) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="conformance", description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    ap.add_argument("--project", type=Path, default=Path.cwd())
    ap.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="the report as JSON")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)
    if W is None:
        print("conformance: bin/warmup could not be loaded -- could not run", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    try:
        data = measure(args.project)
    except CouldNotRun as exc:
        print(f"conformance: {shown(exc)} -- could not run", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    print(json.dumps(data, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False) if args.json else text(data))
    return exit_code(data)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
